# flagcontract — a flag hactl offers is a flag hactl honours: it appears on the
# commands that act on it, it accepts the values it says it accepts, and where
# two inputs name the same thing, passing both ends the command
# (INVARIANTS.md H-25).
#
# The surface is the live cobra tree, walked by sharedFlagSites: every flag name
# MORE THAN ONE command offers — inherited from an ancestor, declared again
# lower down, or both. A flag one command declares and nothing else sees is on
# the command that reads it by construction; there is nowhere for it to be inert
# and nothing for it to disagree with. Every defect behind this file needed a
# second command:
#
#   - `--since` was a root persistent flag: 112 commands offered it, nine read
#     it. `area ls --since garbage-value-xyz` exited 0 with output byte-identical
#     to `area ls`, and six more families did the same, while `log` and
#     `changes` refused the identical value — so whether a mistyped window was an
#     error depended on which command you happened to be running (#54). It is now
#     declared by its nine readers, and the tree refuses it everywhere else with
#     the flag's address in the message.
#   - `--pattern` sat on five listings, four case-insensitive and one that
#     stopped being so in a *consistency* commit (D-2).
#   - `--raw` names a FORMAT on `dash show` and CONTENT on `config file` — one
#     conflicts with `--json`, the other composes with it (D-26).
#   - `--color` is a documented global no-op every command inherits AND a real
#     value-taking flag on `label create` that shadows it. Nothing anywhere said
#     so until this file existed.
#
# What this ledger cannot see, and does not pretend to: the DOMAIN half of H-25
# is closed separately, by globalFlagDomains (internal/cmd/flagcontract.go) over
# the root's own measurable flags, and by TestEveryNumericGlobalFlagStatesItsDomain
# over the live flag set — because a value out of range is a property of the flag
# rather than of the commands that share it.
#
# A `debt` row here is usually one of two shapes, and both are worth reading as
# work rather than as noise: a flag whose declaring commands all act on it while
# no single gate says so, and a flag whose spelling carries two meanings that
# would be a breaking CLI change to separate.
#ceiling 8

--allow-partial = proven: TestPartialScopeSurfaceIsClosed
--area          = proven: TestFilterFlagsAgreeOnCase
--color         = debt: two meanings, one spelling — the root's is a documented no-op every command inherits, `label create`'s is the label's own colour and silently shadows it. Recorded rather than fixed: renaming either is a breaking CLI change.
--component     = proven: TestFilterSurfaceIsClosed
--confirm       = proven: TestConfirmSurfaceIsClosed
--data          = debt: `svc call` and `config flow-step` both take a JSON object and each validates it against a different schema (D-20 for the service, HA's flow schema for the step); no gate holds the two readings to one meaning.
--dir           = proven: TestLoad_ExplicitDirNamesThePathItTried
--domain        = debt: three listings filter by it and `tpl create` names the domain to WRITE — one referent, two verbs. The filters are held to the D-2 pole by TestFilterFlagsAgreeOnCase; nothing gates the pair.
--failing       = proven: TestEveryNarrowedListingSaysWhatNarrowedIt
--file          = debt: eleven commands read an input document from it. `tpl eval`'s was the one that named the same input as a positional and is refused since H-25; no gate quantifies over the other ten.
--full          = proven: TestFullNeverTruncates
--icon          = debt: four registry creates pass it to Home Assistant unchanged and each asserts its own round trip; no gate holds the four to one meaning.
--json          = proven: TestJSONContract
--label         = proven: TestFilterFlagsAgreeOnCase
--level         = debt: two meanings, one spelling — `companion logs --level` is a minimum log level, `floor create --level` is the storey number. Both are established CLI surface.
--name          = proven: TestFilterFlagsAgreeOnCase
--options       = proven: TestFlowLookupErrorExplainsThe404Everywhere
--pattern       = proven: TestGlobIsAnchoredAtEveryIdentifierFormTheListingPrints
--raw           = proven: TestOutputFormatSurfaceIsClosed
--restored      = proven: TestEveryNarrowedListingSaysWhatNarrowedIt
--since         = proven: TestSinceIsDeclaredOnlyOnTheCommandsThatReadIt
--stats         = proven: TestVersionStats
--timeout       = proven: TestTransportSurfaceIsClosed
--tokens        = proven: TestRunWithOutput_TokensFlag
--tokensmax     = proven: TestProseOutputIsStillTokenCapped
--top           = debt: honoured by every command that renders a table and inert on the rest — clause 1's other half, which `--since` closed and this flag has not. Moving it to its consumers is the same change and would take `--full` with it; deliberately not made here.
--tunnel        = exempt: declared once, on the `companion wireguard` group, and inherited by four subcommands that each address a tunnel. The group itself only prints help, so there is no second declaration and no second meaning to reconcile.
--unique        = debt: `log --unique` and `cc logs --unique` deduplicate through the same analyze path and only the `log` side is asserted.
